# Perl/perl5. t/re/re_tests, from the release matching the perl on this
# machine: 5.40.1.
#
# Which is to say this pin was not chosen - it followed what Debian 13 ships
# (5.40.1-6+deb13u1, a current security build, not a stale one). Worth
# knowing before raising it, checked 2026-09-20:
#
#   - 5.40.1 carries UCD 15.0.0. These tables are 17.0.0, so three
#     segmentation rules exist here that this perl has never seen: GB9c
#     (Unicode 15.1), LB15c (16) and LB21a's HH class (17). Forty-six rows of
#     tests/data/vectors/perl/boundaries.rxt are excluded for that reason,
#     each named in tools/corpus/make_boundary_vectors.py.
#   - 5.44.0 is the current stable release and carries UCD 17.0.0 - an exact
#     match with tools/unicode/UCD_VERSION. Raising the pin to it would
#     retire those forty-six exclusions.
#   - The direction matters more than the distance. An oracle *behind* the
#     tables is harmless and documented: the newer rules simply do not fire
#     for it. An oracle *ahead* of them would disagree across the whole
#     corpus, which is what documentation/unicode.md section 1 warns about.
#     So if this moves, UCD_VERSION moves with it or before it, never after.
#
# A second reason, found 2026-09-20 and unrelated to Unicode: 5.40.1 carries a
# known branch reset defect. Perl/perl5#24577, "Regex branch reset (?|...)
# fails with backrefs" - a regression introduced in 5.38, present through
# 5.44, closed 2026-07-22. Its own reproducer on this machine gives the wrong
# answer:
#
#   perl -e 'print "abb" =~ s{^(?|(a)|(b)) (?|(a)|(b)) ((\2)*)}{${&}z}xr'
#   # abzb here; abbz on 5.36 and earlier
#
# The same family shows up in the conditional, where perl contradicts its own
# capture report:
#
#   perl -e 'print "b" =~ /(?|(a)|(b))/ ? "g1=$1" : "no"'   # g1=b
#   perl -e 'print "bx" =~ /(?|(a)|(b))(?(1)x|y)/ ? 1 : 0'  # 0
#   perl -e 'print "by" =~ /(?|(a)|(b))(?(1)x|y)/ ? 1 : 0'  # 1
#
# Group 1 is "b", and the conditional testing it takes the false arm.
# pcre2test 10.46 takes "bx", and so does this library. Two rows of
# tests/data/vectors/known-gaps.txt are the named form of that question and
# say to re-check when this pin moves. Searched the tracker rather than
# reporting anything; #24577 was already there and already fixed.
perl v5.40.1
